===Etymology===
From {{derived|en|ar|عِيد اَلْفِطْر||[[festival|Festival]] of [[breaking|Breaking]] the [[fast|Fast]]}} in reference to the previous avoidance of food from sunrise to sunset during [[Ramadan]]. {{root|en|ar|ع ي د|ف ط ر}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌiːd æl ˈfiːtəɹ/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-Eid al-Fitr.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun|head=[[Eid]] al-Fitr}}

# {{lb|en|Islam}} The [[religious]] [[celebration]] at the end of [[Ramadan]], on the first day of the tenth month of the [[Muslim]] [[lunar]] [[calendar]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Barbara DuMoulin; Sylvia Sikundar|title=Celebrating Our Cultures: Language Arts Activities for Classroom Teachers|location=Markham, Ont.|publisher=Pembroke Publishers|year=1998|page=27|isbn=978-1-55138-102-2|passage='''Eid ul-Fitr''' is one of the main celebrations in the Islamic calendar. {{...}} Prior to '''Eid ul-Fitr''' celebrations people give an extra donation of food or money that is distributed to needy people so that no one will be without the means to celebrate the event. This donation is called ''Zakat ul-Fitr''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Aisha Karen Khan|title=What You Will See Inside a Mosque|location=Woodstock, Vt.|publisher=SkyLight Paths Publishing|year=2003|page=20|isbn=978-1-893361-60-7|passage=At the end of Ramadan, Muslims celebrate one of the most important Islamic holidays, '''''Eid al-fitr'''''. It is a one-day celebration after the last day of fasting. In the evening on the twenty-ninth day of Ramadan, we go out to see if the moon has appeared. If the moon is not seen, we fast one more day. If it is seen, the next day is '''''Eid al-fitr'''''. During '''''Eid al-fitr''''', we visit friends and family and have people over to our houses. It is a very happy and special holiday for children.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Kathleen Tracy|title=Islamic Culture in the Middle East in Perspective|series=World Cultures in Perspective|location=Hockessin, De.|publisher=Mitchell Lane Publishers, Inc.|year=2015|page=27|isbn=978-1-61228-567-2|passage=At the end of Ramadan, there is an exuberant celebration called '''Eid al-Fitr''', the Festival of Fast-Breaking. {{...}} '''Eid al-Fitr''' falls on the first day of Shawwal, the month that follows Ramadan in the Islamic calendar. In the morning, Muslims gather early in outdoor locations or mosques to perform the Eid prayer, which is a short congregational prayer followed by a sermon. After the sermon is finished, everyone leaves to visit family and friends. It is common to bring gifts, especially to children. The celebration can last as long as three days.}}

